## Building Stack[T]

### The Generic Type

```xiom
type Stack[T] {
  items: Vec[T];
}
```

`Stack[T]` is a stack of any type T. The `[T]` makes it generic.

### Generic Methods

```xiom
fn Stack.push[T](&mut self, item: T) {
  self.items.push(item);
}

fn Stack.pop[T](&mut self) -> Option[T] {
  if self.items.len() == 0 {
    return Option.None;
  };
  let last = self.items.len() - 1;
  let result = self.items.get(last).unwrap().clone();
  self.items.remove(last);
  Option.Some(result)
}

fn Stack.peek[T](&self) -> Option[&T] {
  if self.items.len() == 0 {
    return Option.None;
  };
  let last = self.items.len() - 1;
  Option.Some(self.items.get(last).unwrap())
}

fn Stack.size[T](&self) -> Int {
  self.items.len()
}
```

### Using Different Types

```xiom
let mut int_stack: Stack[Int] = Stack{ items: Vec[Int].new() };
int_stack.push(1);
int_stack.push(2);

let mut str_stack: Stack[Str] = Stack{ items: Vec[Str].new() };
str_stack.push("hello");
str_stack.push("world");
```

Same code, different types. The compiler generates type-specific versions automatically.

